CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ nested converting ctor was selected: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ wrapped value is correct: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG: must fail: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [over.ics.user]: a user-defined conversion sequence contains at most one
user-defined conversion, but evaluating whether a converting *template*
constructor is viable (via an is_constructible-style constraint) is a separate,
nested query about a possibly-different target type.  user_defined_conversion_
sequence guarded its converting-template-constructor fallback with a single
global boolean `in_template_conversion`, which blocked ALL nesting: converting
`x -> opt<Wrap>` set the flag, so the nested `x -> Wrap` conversion needed to
evaluate `is_constructible<Wrap, U>` was skipped, the constraint wrongly
evaluated to false, and every `opt<Wrap>` constructor was rejected ("found no
match for symbol 'opt'").  This is how
`std::optional<std::reference_wrapper<const T>>(x)` failed to compile.  Fixed by
keying the recursion guard on the destination type
(template_conversions_in_progress) so a distinct nested target is allowed while
same-target recursion is still cut.  assertion.3 provides non-vacuity.  (Note:
src/util/simplify_utils.cpp additionally needs a fix to is_trivially_
destructible for std::pair before it fully compiles; that is a separate gap.)
